## 🚨 CRITICAL GAPS — Missing from PHANTOM

### GAP TIER 1: Used by 90%+ of Bug Bounty Hunters (HIGH PRIORITY)

---

#### 1. 🚫 Traffic Interception / Proxy
**What pros use:** Burp Suite Pro, OWASP ZAP, Caido
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing — PHANTOM fires automated CLI tools but CANNOT intercept, replay, or manually manipulate live HTTP traffic.

**Why it matters:** Every single bug bounty hunter uses a proxy as their primary workspace. Without it:
- Cannot test business logic (needs manual manipulation)
- Cannot test multi-step auth flows
- Cannot modify request headers on-the-fly
- Cannot do authenticated scanning properly

**Fix for PHANTOM:** Integrate a headless proxy like `mitmproxy` to capture and replay requests programmatically. The agent can then script session replay.

---

#### 2. 🚫 CORS Misconfiguration Scanner
**What pros use:** `corsy`, `cors-scanner`, Burp CORS plugin
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing dedicated — nuclei has some CORS templates but no deep tester

**Why it matters:** CORS misconfigurations are one of the highest-yield bugs on HackerOne. Takes minutes to find, pays $500-$5000.

**Payloads needed:**
```
Origin: null
Origin: https://attacker.com
Origin: https://evil.victim.com
```

---

#### 3. 🚫 Race Condition Testing
**What pros use:** Turbo Intruder (Burp extension), `racepwn`, custom httpx scripts
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

**Why it matters:** Race conditions in payment systems, coupons, and OTP verification = critical severity, massive payouts. Made famous by James Kettle (PortSwigger).

---

#### 4. 🚫 HTTP Request Smuggling
**What pros use:** `smuggler.py`, `h2csmuggler`, Burp HTTP Smuggler extension
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

**Why it matters:** HTTP Request Smuggling can lead to account takeover, cache poisoning, and WAF bypass. Critical severity in virtually all programs.

---

#### 5. 🚫 OAuth Misconfiguration Testing
**What pros use:** Manual + `oauth-scan`, custom scripts, Burp
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing dedicated

**Common OAuth bugs (all manually tested currently):**
- `redirect_uri` bypass
- `state` parameter CSRF
- Code reuse attacks
- Token leakage via Referer

---

#### 6. 🚫 2FA/MFA Bypass Testing
**What pros use:** Manual techniques, Burp Repeater
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

**Common bypasses:**
- OTP reuse (no expiry)
- Response manipulation (`"success": false` → `true`)
- Rate limit bypass on OTP endpoint
- Backup code brute force

---

#### 7. 🚫 Account Takeover (ATO) Workflow
**What pros use:** Combination of manual + targeted payloads
**What PHANTOM has:** No dedicated ATO module

**ATO vectors missing from PHANTOM:**
- Password reset token predictability
- Host header injection in reset emails
- Username enumeration via timing/response
- Email parameter pollution in registration

---

#### 8. 🚫 Web Cache Poisoning / Deception
**What pros use:** `Param Miner` (Burp), manual + James Kettle's methodology
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

**Why it matters:** Cache poisoning = XSS/RCE at scale for all users of a site. Cache deception = steal authenticated user data.

---

#### 9. 🚫 Out-of-Band (OAST) Callback Infrastructure
**What pros use:** `interactsh` (ProjectDiscovery), `webhook.site`, `canarytokens.org`, Burp Collaborator
**What PHANTOM has:** Uses interactsh in some nuclei templates, but NO dedicated OAST server/client

**Why it matters:** Blind SSRF, blind XSS, blind SQLi, blind XXE — ALL require an out-of-band callback to confirm exploitation. Without OAST, PHANTOM will miss a massive class of blind vulnerabilities.

**Fix:** Integrate `interactsh-client` natively. Every scan that touches SSRF, XXE, or blind injection must have a live OAST listener.

---

#### 10. 🚫 Secrets / API Key Leak Scanner
**What pros use:** `truffleHog`, `gitleaks`, `git-dumper`, `KeyHacks`, `nuclei` (secret templates)
**What PHANTOM has:** No dedicated secrets scanner

**What to scan:**
- GitHub repos of the target company
- JS files exposed on the site
- `.git` folder exposed on web servers
- S3 bucket contents
- Docker image layers

**Common high-value leaks:**
- AWS keys (`AKIA...`)
- Stripe/Twilio/Sendgrid API keys
- JWT secrets
- Database credentials in env files

---

### GAP TIER 2: Used by serious bug bounty hunters (MEDIUM PRIORITY)

---

#### 11. 🚫 OSINT Search Engine Integration
**What pros use:** Shodan, Censys, ZoomEye, crt.sh, VirusTotal, hunter.io, intelx.io, fofa.info
**What PHANTOM has:** No API integrations for any of these

**Why it matters:** Find exposed admin panels, open databases (Elasticsearch, MongoDB), old SSL certs with subdomains, employee emails for phishing/social engineering.

**Specific missing integrations:**
- `Shodan API` → find exposed services, versions, CVEs
- `crt.sh` → subdomain discovery via SSL cert transparency
- `hunter.io` → employee email patterns for phishing
- `VirusTotal` → passive DNS, historical URLs

---

#### 12. 🚫 Insecure Deserialization Testing
**What pros use:** `ysoserial` (Java), `PHPGGC` (PHP), `marshal_bruter` (Python)
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

**Why it matters:** Deserialization RCE is critical severity and extremely common in Java enterprise apps, PHP frameworks, and Python Pickle.

---

#### 13. 🚫 Prototype Pollution Scanner
**What pros use:** `ppmap`, custom payloads, Burp
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing dedicated (some nuclei templates exist)

---

#### 14. 🚫 XXE Injection Testing
**What pros use:** Manual + `xxeinjector`, custom OOB payloads
**What PHANTOM has:** nuclei has some XXE templates but no dedicated XXE engine

---

#### 15. 🚫 GraphQL Deep Security Testing
**What pros use:** `graphw00f`, `clairvoyance`, `graphql-voyager`, `InQL` (Burp)
**What PHANTOM has:** Basic graphql scanner but no:
- Introspection abuse
- Batch query DoS
- Field suggestion enumeration
- Nested query depth attacks

---

#### 16. 🚫 SSRF-Specific Deep Testing
**What pros use:** Custom payloads + OAST callbacks, `ssrfmap`, `gopherus`
**What PHANTOM has:** Nuclei SSRF templates (basic) but no:
- Cloud metadata endpoint probing (169.254.169.254, IMDSv2)
- Protocol smuggling (gopher://, dict://, file://)
- Internal network pivoting via SSRF

---

#### 17. 🚫 JWT Attack Suite
**What pros use:** `jwt_tool`, `jwt.io`, Burp JWT editor
**What PHANTOM has:** Basic JWT analysis but missing:
- `alg: none` attack
- Algorithm confusion (RS256 → HS256)
- JWKS injection
- Kid parameter injection (SQLi/LFI)

---

#### 18. 🚫 Wordlist Management System
**What pros use:** SecLists (Daniel Miessler), AssetNote wordlists, custom company-specific lists
**What PHANTOM has:** No wordlist management — assumes system wordlists exist

**Fix:** Build a wordlist manager that:
- Auto-downloads SecLists and AssetNote lists on setup
- Generates target-specific wordlists from crawled content
- Has tiered wordlists (quick/medium/thorough) per scan mode

---

#### 19. 🚫 Mobile Application Security
**What pros use:** `MobSF`, `Frida`, `objection`, `jadx`, `apktool`, `dex2jar`
**What PHANTOM has:** `ghidra` (general binary), NO mobile-specific tooling

**Missing:**
- APK/IPA static analysis
- Runtime hooking with Frida
- SSL pinning bypass
- Deep link testing
- Exported activity testing

---

#### 20. 🚫 WordPress / CMS-Specific Scanners
**What pros use:** `WPScan`, `CMSeeK`, `droopescan`
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing CMS-specific

**Why it matters:** Massive percentage of bug bounty targets run WordPress. WPScan finds plugin/theme CVEs automatically.

---

### GAP TIER 3: Red Team / Advanced Operations (LOWER PRIORITY for bug bounty, HIGH for red team)

---

#### 21. 🚫 Active Directory / Internal Network
**What red teamers use:** `BloodHound`/`SharpHound`, `Impacket`, `CrackMapExec`/`NetExec`, `Responder`, `Evil-WinRM`, `Rubeus`, `Mimikatz`
**What PHANTOM has:** Basic `enum4linux`, `netexec` integration (partial)

**Missing:**
- BloodHound graph ingestion + attack path analysis
- Kerberoasting / ASREProasting
- Pass-the-Hash / Pass-the-Ticket
- LDAP enumeration (via `ldapdomaindump`)
- DCSync attack
- Lateral movement playbooks

---

#### 22. 🚫 C2 Framework Integration
**What red teamers use:** Cobalt Strike, Sliver, Havoc, Mythic, Metasploit
**What PHANTOM has:** Metasploit (basic `msfvenom` and `msfconsole`) but no:
- Automated beacon deployment
- C2 listener management
- Post-exploitation automation
- Malleable C2 profiles for evasion

---

#### 23. 🚫 Payload Obfuscation / WAF Evasion
**What pros use:** `Veil`, custom encoders, `donut`, `shellter`
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing for EDR/WAF evasion

**Missing:**
- Payload encoding/obfuscation pipeline
- WAF fingerprinting + bypass per WAF type (Cloudflare, Akamai, ModSecurity)
- Traffic shaping to look human

---

#### 24. 🚫 Cloud Attack Surface
**What cloud pentesters use:** `ScoutSuite`, `Prowler`, `pacu` (AWS), `roadrecon` (Azure AD), `gcp_scanner`
**What PHANTOM has:** `prowler` and `trivy` (partial) but missing:
- AWS IAM privilege escalation paths (`pacu`)
- Azure AD enumeration (`roadrecon`, `AADInternals`)
- GCP service account abuse
- S3/Blob/GCS bucket misconfiguration scanner

---

#### 25. 🚫 Phishing / Social Engineering Infrastructure
**What red teamers use:** `GoPhish`, `Evilginx2`, `Modlishka`
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

---

### GAP TIER 4: 2024-2025 Emerging Threats (FUTURE)

---

#### 26. 🚫 AI/LLM Security Testing
**What's emerging:** Prompt injection, model extraction, data poisoning, jailbreaking, LLM agent hijacking
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

**Why it matters:** Every major company is shipping LLM-powered features in 2025. Bug bounty programs are starting to include AI attack surfaces. This is the NEXT big category.

**Payloads needed:**
- Prompt injection in chatbots and AI features
- Indirect prompt injection via document upload
- Model inversion / membership inference attacks
- Training data extraction

---

#### 27. 🚫 Browser Automation / Headless Hacking
**What pros use:** `Playwright`, `Puppeteer`, `selenium`
**What PHANTOM has:** No headless browser at all

**Missing capabilities:**
- Authenticated crawling (login then crawl)
- JavaScript-rendered page analysis
- DOM XSS that only triggers in real browser
- Cookie/session theft via XSS verification
- SPAs (React/Angular/Vue) that don't work with HTTP-only crawlers

---

#### 28. 🚫 Dependency Confusion / Supply Chain
**What pros use:** Custom scripts + `confused`, `pip-audit`, `npm audit`
**What PHANTOM has:** Nothing

---

## 📊 Scorecard: PHANTOM vs Real World

| Domain | Industry Standard | PHANTOM Score |
|---|---|---|
| Recon & OSINT | 10/10 | 5/10 ⚠️ |
| Web Fuzzing | 10/10 | 9/10 ✅ |
| Vulnerability Scanning | 10/10 | 7/10 ✅ |
| Web Injection (SQLi/XSS) | 10/10 | 7/10 ✅ |
| Traffic Interception | 10/10 | 0/10 ❌ |
| Business Logic Testing | 10/10 | 1/10 ❌ |
| Race Conditions | 10/10 | 0/10 ❌ |
| OAuth/JWT/SSO | 10/10 | 2/10 ❌ |
| OAST/Blind Vuln Detection | 10/10 | 2/10 ❌ |
| Secrets Scanning | 10/10 | 0/10 ❌ |
| Mobile Security | 10/10 | 0/10 ❌ |
| Active Directory | 10/10 | 2/10 ❌ |
| Cloud Security | 10/10 | 3/10 ⚠️ |
| Evasion / Stealth | 10/10 | 1/10 ❌ |
| AI/LLM Security | 10/10 | 0/10 ❌ |
| Reporting Quality | 10/10 | 4/10 ⚠️ |

**Overall: ~37% coverage of what real professionals use daily.**

